            B.q("ai-m2-1", "ai-acr", "Images older than 30 days that no longer have any tag must be removed every night, with no server of your own. What do you set up?", listOf("Geo-replication of the registry to a cheaper region", "A registry webhook that deletes the previous image on each push", "An ACR task created with az acr task create --schedule \"0 1 * * *\" --context /dev/null whose command is acr purge --filter 'recipes-api:^\$' --untagged --ago 30d", "az acr import with the --force flag, run every night"), setOf(2), "acr purge is a container command (still in preview) that ACR Tasks can run inside Azure. The tag pattern ^\$ matches no tag names, so no tagged image is touched. --untagged then deletes manifests (stored images) that have no tag, and --ago 30d limits that to ones older than 30 days. A filter such as recipes-api:.* would also delete every tag older than 30 days, including release tags still in use. acr purge --untagged-only --ago 30d does the same job as the ^\$ filter. Creating the task with --schedule, a cron expression (a time pattern such as 0 1 * * * for 01:00 daily), runs it on a timer, and --context /dev/null means it needs no source code. ACR Tasks work in every registry tier.",
                scenario = "Fabrikam Recipes' build pipeline pushes a new image of its recipe API to a Basic tier Azure Container Registry for every commit. Storage keeps growing with old images nobody runs.",
